\section{Nested submodels}{
Both single-channel models are reachable from the full kernel by pinning
\code{pi}, so a nested model comparison needs no change of \code{model}:

\describe{
\item{\code{pi = qnorm(0)}}{Pure transient race, identical to
\code{\link[=BTAwLTransient]{BTAwLTransient()}}.}
\item{\code{pi = qnorm(1)}}{Pure sustained race, identical to
\code{\link[=BTAwLSustained]{BTAwLSustained()}}.}
}

At a pinned \code{pi} the other channel's time constant no longer enters the
likelihood, so it must be supplied as a constant --- left free it would
sample against a flat likelihood and never converge.  Any value inside its
bounds gives exactly the same fit; \code{log(1)} is the conventional choice:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{# pure transient                       # pure sustained
constants = c(pi = qnorm(0),           constants = c(pi = qnorm(1),
              tau_s = log(1))                        tau_t = log(1))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{tau_s} and \code{tau_t} are the direct transient/sustained rate parameters;
there is no endpoint parameterisation to switch between.  Do not try to
switch a channel off with \code{tau_s = log(0)} or \code{tau_t = log(0)}.  Both are
outside the bounds, and neither means what it looks like: as \code{tau_s} falls
to zero the sustained drive becomes a \emph{step} at full strength (the fastest
sustained input, not the absence of one), while a vanishing \code{tau_t}
delivers zero total transient impulse.  \code{pi} is the switch.
}

